DNS, LES, RANS and what the computation resolves (@fd-les-dns: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @fluid-dynamics, #source "../sources/Turbulence-McDonough.lingua#r_2TBK3JC1KJ1CMNABRW3Y2AFKTQ", #source "../sources/Turbulence-Modeling-Resource.lingua#r_42VW512D9MG3Z0SBJ78N7DAPE6", #source "../sources/Johns-Hopkins-Turbulence-Databases.lingua#r_1D25T86CC1CHT4BX098WQXBQPE", #source "../sources/Notes-on-CFD-Greenshields-Weller.lingua#r_50W8G5SX00YH7R6TBXK4FAP5MY", #fluid-dynamics "../../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/properties/Fundamental-property-models-derivatives-and-verification.lingua#r_764YPYD3A83WPVMG0N594Y9W71") { r_7J730ZRGKC9B6BVVHTZK9D2YYE
**Read:** [Introductory Lectures on Turbulence — James M. McDonough](../sources/Turbulence-McDonough.lingua#r_2TBK3JC1KJ1CMNABRW3Y2AFKTQ), numerical approaches and modelling; [Turbulence Modeling Resource — model definitions and benchmarks](../sources/Turbulence-Modeling-Resource.lingua#r_42VW512D9MG3Z0SBJ78N7DAPE6), verification versus validation cases; [Johns Hopkins Turbulence Databases — numerical flow evidence](../sources/Johns-Hopkins-Turbulence-Databases.lingua#r_1D25T86CC1CHT4BX098WQXBQPE), DNS provenance; [Notes on Computational Fluid Dynamics — Greenshields and Weller](../sources/Notes-on-CFD-Greenshields-Weller.lingua#r_50W8G5SX00YH7R6TBXK4FAP5MY), turbulence modelling and numerical methods.

Direct numerical simulation seeks to resolve the relevant continuum dynamics without a turbulence closure, but still has numerical, domain and sampling errors. Large-eddy simulation filters the equations and models unresolved transport. Reynolds-averaged modelling predicts averages through closure assumptions. These are different mathematical targets, not merely low, medium and high mesh counts.

Derive the filtered stress and compare it with Reynolds stress. Explain the filter, grid and model dependence, wall treatment and inflow content of a chosen LES. For DNS, assess the smallest spatial/time scales and statistical duration in the actual flow. For RANS, identify the meaning of steady convergence when the physical flow is unsteady.

Choose a benchmark with documented observables and compare at least two modelling levels against independent evidence. Track numerical convergence and statistical convergence separately. An apparently converged force from a model can remain physically biased; a changing instantaneous field can nevertheless yield a statistically converged mean. Relate the required computational cost to the question being asked.

**Continue:** [Fundamental property models, derivatives and verification](../../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/properties/Fundamental-property-models-derivatives-and-verification.lingua#r_764YPYD3A83WPVMG0N594Y9W71).

